---
source: crates/max/src/mcp/tool/tests.rs
expression: DECLARATIONS.as_str()
---
/** Looks through this conversation's chat records, with the descriptions of pictures and stickers. For things from before the context, who said what, or what was talked about on a day, look before answering. The filters go together:
  who is a few characters of the speaker's name (the group card at the time); from and to are YYYY-MM-DD (a whole day, the day itself included both ways), YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM, or #number (from includes that message, to doesn't).
  With query it searches, and returns results: query is keywords separated by spaces, at most 8, each looked for as written, whatever its case; keep a Chinese keyword short, two or three characters. The whole query is also matched by meaning. Messages with more of the keywords, and nearer in meaning, come first, and of equals the newer; limit is 10 by default and at most 30. text is a passage around the match; read, passed back as it is, reads that message with 10 on each side. Recalled messages aren't searched.
  Without query it reads the messages themselves, a line each in order of time, as in the context, the long ones only their beginning: with from, forward from there; without, the newest (up to to); limit is 50 by default and at most 100. When there is more, next holds the arguments to go on with, passed back as they are: forward from a read with from, backward from a read of the newest. Give at least one of who, from and to.
  around reads that message with before and after messages on each side (10 by default, at most 50), and goes with no other filter; with before and after both 0 it reads that one message alone, whole however long.
  A read comes in pages, none longer than one message may be in the context: pages is how many there are, and page picks one. What /clear --all cleared is only out of the context, and is found all the same. */
history(args: { query?: string; who?: string; from?: string; to?: string; around?: number; before?: number; after?: number; limit?: number; page?: number }): Promise<{ results: { ref: string; time: string; text: string; read: { around: number } }[] } | { text: string; page: number; pages: number; next?: object }>;

/** Searches the web: the first results' titles, links, summaries and publication dates, where known. For facts, prices, news, plots and whatever else you aren't sure of or may be out of date, search before answering, and don't go by memory.
  limit is 1 to 10, 5 by default. For several searches use Promise.all. To read a page in full, run webread <url> with shell. */
web_search(args: { query: string; limit?: number }): Promise<{ results: { title: string; url: string; snippet: string; published?: string }[] }>;

/** Sends a picture under /work to this chat: a chart you drew, a screenshot, a downloaded picture. PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP, at most 10 MiB; no symlink on the path.
  caption is a line under the picture, in the same message. It returns once the picture is sent, and handle is the handle of that picture in its message.
  A picture or sticker the records already have needs none of this: write its handle in your reply. */
send_image(args: { path: string; caption?: string }): Promise<{ handle: string }>;

/** Sends a file under /work to this chat, where it shows as a file that opens to download: documents, spreadsheets, PDFs, archives, code. At most 200 MiB, and not empty; no symlink on the path.
  name is the file name shown in the chat, the path's by default. It returns once the file is stored and queued, and handle is the file's handle; the upload goes on after that, a big file takes a minute or two to reach the chat, and what you say afterwards waits behind it. If it doesn't go out, its row in the records is marked （没发出去）.
  A picture for everyone to see goes with send_image, not as a file. To send again a file the records already have, copy it to /work with max.file first. */
send_file(args: { path: string; name?: string }): Promise<{ handle: string }>;

/** Recalls a message of this chat; id is the number in the #number at the start of its line. Whether to is yours to judge: for flooding, ads or what breaks the rules, or when someone asks and the reason is sound; say so briefly afterwards.
  QQ lets only messages sent within 2 minutes be recalled: what you said yourself (a segment is a message, so a reply sent in several is recalled one at a time), and, when you are an admin of the group, an ordinary member's; the owner's and the admins' can't be. In a private chat only your own.
  It returns once QQ has recalled it; if QQ doesn't, the call fails and says why. The records gain a row of yours, [unsend#id], marked （没发出去） if QQ didn't recall it. */
recall_message(args: { id: number }): Promise<{ recalled: number }>;

/** Mutes someone in this group, or lifts a mute. member is the number in [mention#number]; seconds is how long from now, 0 lifts it, at most 2592000 (30 days). Whether to mute, and for how long, is yours to judge: for flooding, ads or abuse, or when someone asks and the reason is sound; say so briefly in the chat afterwards.
  QQ lets only a group's admins mute, and only ordinary members: the owner, the admins and yourself can't be muted, there is no muting in a private chat, and nobody who hasn't spoken in this group can be muted. Muting someone already muted moves the end to seconds from now.
  It returns once QQ has done it: until is when the mute ends, null when lifted; if QQ doesn't, the call fails and says why. No row is added to the records. */
mute_member(args: { member: number; seconds: number }): Promise<{ member: number; until: string | null }>;

/** Finds stickers in the library by meaning: the library holds the stickers seen in every chat, ranked by how near each description is to what query means, nearest first.
  It finds and doesn't send: pick one that fits and copy its handle into your reply as [sticker#…], and it goes out; if none fits, send none. limit is 1 to 10, 5 by default. */
find_stickers(args: { query: string; limit?: number }): Promise<{ stickers: { handle: string; description: string }[] }>;

/** Starts a background agent on something that takes time: looking up a pile of sources, reading many pages or files, writing code and running experiments. It runs in this chat's VM and shares /work with you; it has its own shell, file reading and writing, history and web_search; it can't speak in the chat, nor send pictures or files, recall or mute.
  It doesn't see the conversation, only task: so put in the background, what to do, how far to take it, and what the report should say. title is a one-line title, shown by status.
  workdir is the directory it starts in, /work by default: an absolute path, or one relative to /work; made if it isn't there. For work on a project give the project's path: it reads the project's AGENTS.md (or CLAUDE.md) and the notes background agents keep for that directory (under /work/.max/projects/), and updates the notes when done.
  Returns { agent: "agent#3" } at once, and you go on talking. Stuck on something only you or the people here know, it asks you: the question appears as [ask#5 问] agent#3 <title>, and you answer it with answer, to 5. It may also ask the conversations you may ask. When it is done, its report appears among your new messages as [agent#3 完成], the whole of it saved under /work/.max/jobs/, and what to say of it in the chat is yours to decide. At most 3 at once, each for at most 2 hours; at its time it stops and hands over what it has. */
agent(args: { task: string; title?: string; workdir?: string }): Promise<{ agent: string }>;

/** Tells a running background agent something more: a change of direction, more information, what someone here said. It sees it after the tool call it is on, before it next thinks. agent is "agent#3" or 3.
  A question it asked you ([ask#5 问] agent#3 <title>) is answered with answer, to 5, not with this: answered, it stops waiting. Once it has nothing else to do, it waits at most 10 minutes for the answer, then goes on by its own judgement. */
agent_steer(args: { agent: string | number; message: string }): Promise<{ agent: string }>;

/** Stops a background agent (agent#3) or a background command of shell's (shell#4) whose result isn't wanted any more: nothing comes back from it, and what it left in /work stays. Also withdraws a question you asked another conversation (ask#5) that still waits for its answer: an answer given there afterwards finds nobody. A question asked of you isn't cancelled: answer it, or leave it unanswered. */
cancel(args: { handle: string }): Promise<{ cancelled: string }>;

/** Shows what runs in the background: agents (agent#3); commands of shell's (shell#4), titled $ and the command; and questions you asked another conversation that still wait for the answer (ask#5), titled ? with the conversation and the question. Each with its handle, title and minutes run; an agent with its model calls (calls), its tool calls (tools) and the tool it last used, the others with 0 and none. Without handle, all of them; those done, cancelled or answered aren't listed, nor are the questions asked of you. */
status(args: { handle?: string }): Promise<{ running: { handle: string; title: string; minutes: number; calls: number; tools: number; tool: string | null }[] }>;

/** Asks Max in another conversation (another group, or a private chat) a question. Only those listed under [能问的会话] can be asked; to is the number, 9 for c#9, and you always give it: only a background agent leaves it out, to ask you. With no such block in the context, nobody can be asked from here.
  Max there answers by its own rules, with its own records, memory and tools; whether to answer and how much is its to decide. It doesn't see this conversation, nor you its, so question has to carry the background and what is asked in full, in at most 2000 characters; a [mention#…], a message number or a picture's handle from here means nothing there: say it with names and words.
  Neither the question nor the answer is sent to either chat; only the two Maxes see them. The people here don't know you asked: whether to say so, and how to pass the answer on, is yours to decide.
  Returns { ask: "ask#3" } at once, and you go on talking. When it answers, the answer appears among your new messages as [ask#3 回答], with what you asked; if no answer comes, as [ask#3 没答成] and why. It may ask its own people first, and is waited for at most 2 hours. A question that waits shows in status, its title starting with ?; to stop waiting, withdraw it with cancel. Your background agents may ask the same conversations: their questions show there too, the title ending with (agent#3), and the answer goes to the agent. */
ask(args: { to?: number; question: string }): Promise<{ ask: string }>;

/** Answers a question asked of you: by Max in another conversation, when your new messages have [ask#5 问] c#7 <name> and the question, or by one of your background agents, [ask#5 问] agent#3 <title>. to is that number, 5 for ask#5, and text is the answer, at most 2000 characters.
  Another conversation's Max is not anyone here: it doesn't see this conversation, only your text. What may be said, and how much, is yours to judge by the rules and memory of this conversation; you may ask the people here first and answer once you know, and it waits at most 2 hours. You may leave it unanswered, or answer that you'd rather not say. An agent asks what only you or the people here know; once it has nothing else to do, it waits at most 10 minutes more, then goes on by its own judgement.
  Neither the question nor your answer is sent to this chat, so the people here don't know anyone asked: whether to tell them is yours to decide. A question still unanswered when this turn ends is listed under [等你回答的问题] in the next. Questions are numbered here whichever way they go: one you asked yourself is answered to you, not by you. A question is answered once; if nobody waits any more (withdrawn, out of time, the agent ended, or Max has restarted), the call fails. Returns whom it went to: { to: "c#7 <name>" }, or { to: "agent#3 <title>" }. */
answer(args: { to: number; text: string }): Promise<{ to: string }>;

/** Shows or sets a few of this chat's settings. Without arguments it only shows; one given is set, and 0 puts it back to the default. Returns each one's value now and its default. Whether to change one is yours to judge: when someone asks and the reason holds, or when you do need it; say so briefly afterwards. A group's admins can set them too, with /config, and the last to set one wins.
  idle_window_secs: how many seconds after you last spoke an idle turn ends (60–7200). Called again within the window, you still have what this turn found; called after it, a new turn starts and reads from the records again. The default follows the model. In force at once, for this turn too.
  call_time_secs: how many seconds a program or a command waits at most for each call of a Max tool, max.video and max.file among them (10–600). Raise it when converting a long video or copying a big file runs out of time. In force from the next run_code or shell.
  commands_at_once: how many background commands run at once at most (1–16). In force at once. */
config(args: { idle_window_secs?: number; call_time_secs?: number; commands_at_once?: number }): Promise<{ idle_window_secs: { value: number; default: number }; call_time_secs: { value: number; default: number }; commands_at_once: { value: number; default: number } }>;
